  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">UN Peacebuilding Fund 2020-24</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Peacebuilding Fund (PBF) is the UN&#x2019;s primary instrument dedicated to sustaining peace and preventing violent conflict. Managed by the Peacebuilding and Peace Support Office in the UN Secretariat, the PBF offers rapid and flexible support to nationally led efforts that strengthen social cohesion, political dialogue, and institutional resilience.

The PBF&#x2019;s 2020&#x2013;2026 Strategy, extended through 2026, aligns with the Pact for the Future and New Agenda for Peace, focusing on four interconnected thematic pillars:

The first pillar, Women, Peace, and Security, advances gender equality and women&#x2019;s leadership in peace processes, transitional justice, and local governance, implementing Security Council Resolution 1325.
The second, Youth, Peace, and Security, promotes youth engagement through mediation, civic participation, and reconciliation, education, economic empowerment, and livelihoods.
The third pillar, Cross-Border and Regional Cooperation, fosters dialogue where instability crosses borders, addressing shared challenges tied to climate, displacement, and natural resources. 

Finally, PBF supports the development of national prevention strategies so governments can identify and tackle conflict risks through integrated early warning, inclusion, and institutional reform.

Across all pillars, the PBF emphasizes context-sensitive, inclusive, and adaptive approaches rooted in local partnerships, working closely with civil society, particularly women&#x2019;s and youth-led organizations, while using its Peacebuilding Impact Hub to capture lessons and scale effective models.

In 2025, the Fund supported work in Bosnia and Herzegovina, Burundi, Cameroon, the Central African Republic, Colombia, the Democratic Republic of the Congo, El Salvador, The Gambia, Guatemala, Guinea-Bissau, Haiti, Honduras, Kyrgyzstan, Lebanon, Liberia, Libya, Madagascar, Malawi, Mali, Mauritania, Papua New Guinea, Senegal, Sierra Leone, Somalia, Solomon Islands, South Sudan, Togo and Tunisia.</narrative>
  </description>
